<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ghezo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1818–1858</span> · Also known as Gezo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ghezo was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, from 1818 to 1858. He came to power in a coup against his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adandozan">Adandozan</a>, ended Dahomey's tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a> in 1823 and expanded the army, including the corps of women soldiers. Dahomey's economy depended heavily on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, and under British pressure he began to promote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> exports.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ghezo.jpg" alt="Ghezo" width="320" height="543" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Forbes, Frederick E. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gezo_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
